          "path": "internal/sessionkit/open.go",
          "lineNumber": 19,
          "lineContent": "func OpenSessions(ctx context.Context, cfg config.Config) (*sessions.Store, func(), error) {",
          "title": "Open sessions store",
          "description": "Opens sessions store with cleanup"
        },
        {
          "id": "5b",
          "path": "internal/sessionkit/open.go",
          "lineNumber": 32,
          "lineContent": "func OpenTasks(ctx context.Context, cfg config.Config) (tasks.Store, func(), error) {",
          "title": "Open tasks store",
          "description": "Opens tasks store with cleanup"
        },
        {
          "id": "5c",
          "path": "internal/sessionkit/open.go",
          "lineNumber": 45,
          "lineContent": "func OpenMemory(ctx context.Context, cfg config.Config) (*memory.Store, func(), error) {",
          "title": "Open memory store",
          "description": "Opens memory store with cleanup"
        },
        {
          "id": "5d",
          "path": "internal/sessionkit/open.go",
          "lineNumber": 58,
          "lineContent": "func OpenMemoryInCache(ctx context.Context, cfg config.Config) (*memory.Store, func(), error) {",
          "title": "Open memory in cache",
          "description": "Opens memory store in cache path for ephemeral data"
        },
        {
          "id": "5e",
          "path": "internal/sessionkit/open.go",
          "lineNumber": 24,
          "lineContent": "cleanup := func() { _ = store.Close() }",
          "title": "Store cleanup pattern",
          "description": "Provides cleanup function for store resources"
        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "Storage Layer Integration\n├── sessionkit package provides utilities\n│   ├── OpenSessions() \u003c-- 5a\n│   │   ├── sessions.Open(ctx, cfg.Storage.Root) \u003c-- internal/sessionkit/open.go:20\n│   │   └── cleanup closure \u003c-- 5e\n│   ├── OpenTasks() \u003c-- 5b\n│   │   ├── tasks.Open(ctx, cfg.Storage.Root) \u003c-- internal/sessionkit/open.go:33\n│   │   └── cleanup closure \u003c-- 5e\n│   ├── OpenMemory() \u003c-- 5c\n│   │   ├── memory.Open(ctx, cfg.Storage.Root, cfg.Paths.CAS) \u003c-- internal/sessionkit/open.go:46\n│   │   └── cleanup closure \u003c-- 5e\n│   └── OpenMemoryInCache() \u003c-- 5d\n│       ├── memory.Open(ctx, cfg.Paths.Cache, cfg.Paths.CAS) \u003c-- internal/sessionkit/open.go:59\n│       └── cleanup closure \u003c-- 5e\n└── Config-based path resolution\n    ├── cfg.Storage.Root for persistent storage \u003c-- internal/sessionkit/open.go:20\n    ├── cfg.Paths.Cache for ephemeral data \u003c-- internal/sessionkit/open.go:59\n    └── cfg.Paths.CAS for content-addressable storage \u003c-- internal/sessionkit/open.go:46",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nThe foxctl system needs **consistent storage management** across multiple skills that handle sessions, tasks, and memory. Each skill needs to open database connections, manage cleanup, and resolve paths, but duplicating this logic would create maintenance nightmares and potential resource leaks. The storage layer solves this by providing **centralized store management** with automatic cleanup and consistent path resolution.\n\n## Details\n\nThe sessionkit package provides **five core store opening functions** that handle all storage needs:\n\n- **OpenSessions** [5a] opens the sessions database for storing session metadata and embeddings\n- **OpenTasks** [5b] opens the tasks database for task management and tracking  \n- **OpenMemory** [5c] opens the memory store for semantic search and results\n- **OpenMemoryInCache** [5d] opens memory in the cache directory for **ephemeral data** like session snapshots\n- Each function returns a **cleanup closure** [5e] that ensures proper resource cleanup\n\nThe key pattern is **RAII-style resource management** - every store opening returns both the store and a cleanup function. This prevents resource leaks across the many skills that use storage. The path resolution uses the config system to consistently map between logical storage types (persistent, cache, CAS) and actual filesystem locations.\n\nAll stores use the same underlying database drivers but expose **different interfaces** tailored to their specific use cases - sessions for semantic search, tasks for workflow management, and memory for general-purpose storage with vector capabilities."
    }
  ],
  "description": "Maps the session capture/recall and memory query systems, covering session state persistence, semantic retrieval, and filtered memory access. Key locations include session save orchestration [1a], embedding generation [2c], vector search with BM25 fallback [3c], session ID resolution [4a], and storage layer management [5a].",
